  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>An HSE inspector walks onto your site and asks for the scaffold inspection record. You cannot find it. Work at height stops, on the spot. This is what a <strong>construction site inspection report</strong> must contain under UK law, and what it costs when the paperwork is missing.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>CDM 2015 puts the duty to manage and monitor site safety on the principal contractor. Inspection records are the proof.</li><li>Inspect scaffold before first use, after anything that affects it, and at least every seven days.</li><li>Schedule 7 of the Work at Height Regulations 2005 sets the minimum content of a scaffold report.</li><li>No record is treated as no inspection. That means a Prohibition Notice, or worse after an incident.</li><li>A general site report covers welfare, access, edge protection, plant, electrics and fire.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The duty",
      "heading": "CDM 2015 puts monitoring on the principal contractor",
      "html": "<p>The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015 give the principal contractor a clear job: plan, manage, monitor and coordinate health and safety through the construction phase. That means inspections happen and the results are written down.</p><p>CDM 2015 does not set how often for every item. The principal contractor weighs the risk. The construction phase plan must cover site rules, induction and ongoing monitoring. The record is the proof.</p><p>The principal designer owns the pre-construction phase. When the site goes live, the principal contractor carries the safety duty. If one firm is both, both duties apply.</p><p>For notifiable projects, those over 30 working days with more than 20 workers at once, or over 500 person-days, tell HSE before work starts. The records feed the health and safety file for the client.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Scaffolding",
      "heading": "When a scaffold must be inspected",
      "html": "<p>The Work at Height Regulations 2005 are clear on scaffolding used as a place of work. Regulation 12 needs an inspection before first use, after anything likely to affect stability, such as high winds, heavy snow or a vehicle impact, and at least every seven days while it stays in use.</p><p>A competent person does each inspection. The report is finished before the end of the working period. It stays on site until the scaffold comes down. The employer then keeps it for three months.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Schedule 7",
      "heading": "What a scaffold report must contain",
      "html": "<p>Schedule 7 sets the minimum. Every line is a must.</p><ul><li>Name and address of the person the inspection was for</li><li>Where the working platform is, and what it is</li><li>Date and time</li><li>Anything that could risk health or safety</li><li>Action taken</li><li>Further action needed</li><li>Name and position of the person making the report</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The wider walk",
      "heading": "What a general site report covers",
      "html": "<p>Beyond scaffold, a general report covers the whole site. There is no single set format, but most use the same items.</p><ul><li>Welfare: toilets, washing, drinking water, rest area</li><li>Housekeeping: waste, clear routes, materials storage</li><li>Access: site entrance, people and vehicles kept apart</li><li>Edge protection: barriers at openings, floor holes, roof edges</li><li>Excavations: support, edge protection, ladders, spoil</li><li>Plant: condition, operator certificates, exclusion zones</li><li>Electrics: temporary supplies, cables, RCDs in use</li><li>Fire: extinguishers, escape routes, hot work controls</li><li>PPE: available and worn</li><li>Subcontractors: controls in place, kept apart from other trades</li></ul><p>A weekly inspection by the site manager or safety officer, with daily informal checks, is normal on medium projects. The weekly one gives the written record.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Why records slip",
      "heading": "The gap between the walk and the write-up",
      "html": "<p>The problem is timing. Notes on a clipboard get typed up later. Detail leaks away in the gap.</p><p>Tablet forms fix part of it. They add battery, patchy signal and a new screen to learn.</p><p>With Quickler you run the walk in WhatsApp, the app already on every phone on site. Photos go in the chat, timed. No typing up afterwards. See <a href=\"/pages/how-to-write-site-inspection-report-uk.html\">how to write a site inspection report</a> or the <a href=\"/pages/construction-daily-report-app-uk.html\">construction daily report app</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "When records slip",
      "heading": "No record is treated as no inspection",
      "html": "<p>An HSE inspector can ask for scaffold records at any time. If the last report is missing or out of date, work at height stops.</p><p>A Prohibition Notice can be served on the spot. It halts the activity until a competent person inspects. A lost day on a live job is rarely cheap.</p><p>After a serious incident, missing records are not an admin slip. They suggest safety was not managed. Prosecution under the Work at Height Regulations 2005 or the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 is possible. In civil claims, no records makes it harder to show you took reasonable care. The fix is simple: a current record you can show on demand. For specialist work, see <a href=\"/pages/structural-engineer-report-software-uk.html\">structural engineer report software</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>How often must scaffolding be inspected?</h3><p>Under the Work at Height Regulations 2005, a scaffold used as a place of work is inspected before first use, after anything likely to affect it (such as high winds or an impact), and at least every seven days while in use. Each inspection is recorded. The report is kept until the scaffold comes down.</p><h3>Who can inspect a scaffold?</h3><p>A competent person. That means enough training, experience and knowledge to spot hazards and judge risk. No set qualification is required. Many firms use a CISRS-qualified inspector or a trained site manager.</p><h3>What must a scaffold report contain?</h3><p>Schedule 7 sets the minimum. Who the inspection was for, with address. Where the platform is, and what it is. Date and time. Anything that could cause a risk. Action taken. Further action needed. Name and position of the person who wrote it.</p><h3>What if an inspector asks and you have no record?</h3><p>No record is treated as no inspection. That can mean a Prohibition Notice that stops work, an Improvement Notice, or prosecution. After a serious incident, missing records make your position much worse.</p>"
    }
